        summary: "Recovery needs a committed manifest, not a directory of unrelated rank files.",
        points: [
          "A checkpoint restores a training step, not a model, so anything that would make step 40,001 execute differently the second time belongs under one checkpoint ID. That means the optimizer moments and the scheduler position as much as the weights, and it means the data-loader cursor and RNG state too, since a restart that reshuffles its own data has changed the experiment. Reload the weights alone and the run resumes on a different trajectory while reporting the same step number, which is the hardest class of training bug to see.",
          "Ranks upload immutable shards and checksums; a coordinator publishes the manifest only after every required shard is durable. Incomplete checkpoints remain invisible and are garbage-collected later.",
          "On restart, validate lineage and reshard if the topology changed. Then separate the two failure shapes with different instruments: a straggler shows up as one rank's step time and collective duration diverging from its peers while loss behaves normally, and divergence shows up as gradient norms and loss moving on every rank at once. Watching only the job-level step time reports both as 'training got slower'.",
        ],
