          "One-shot retrieval assumes the user's question is already a good query. Many are not. 'Which of our vendors are affected by the policy we changed in March' needs one retrieval to identify the policy and a second query constructed from what the first returned, and the second document is neither lexically nor semantically near the original question, so no amount of reranking a single candidate list will surface it. Recognizing the multi-hop shape is the design decision; the loop is just the implementation.",
          "The implementations form a spectrum by how much discretion the model gets. At the cheap deterministic end sit query rewriting and decomposition, which turn one question into several sub-queries before any retrieval happens, and hypothetical-answer embedding, which embeds a drafted answer rather than the question because answers live nearer to answers in the vector space. In the middle sits graph retrieval, which follows explicit entity, citation, or containment edges built at ingest, so the next hop is a traversal rather than a guess. At the open end sits agentic retrieval: search is a tool, and the model issues a query, reads the evidence, judges sufficiency, reformulates or drills down, and stops when the question is answered or the budget is gone.",
          "Cost and latency now scale with hops, and hop count is data-dependent, so p99 diverges from p50 far more than in a one-shot system and a single pathological question can cost a hundred times the median. Bound it explicitly: a hard hop cap, a per-request token budget, and a defined behavior at exhaustion that answers with the evidence gathered and says so rather than silently truncating. Deduplicate evidence by chunk identity across hops, because the same chunk retrieved under five phrasings will otherwise fill the context window with one document.",
          "Every hop re-enters the authorization check, and this is where an iterative retriever gets dangerous. A second-hop query built from first-hop content must be filtered against the same point-in-time policy as the first, or an authorized document becomes a stepping stone that reveals the existence and content of an unauthorized one. Retrieved text is now steering the next query as well, which makes prompt injection a retrieval-control problem and not only a tool problem: a planted paragraph can redirect the whole loop toward documents the attacker chose. Log the full query trajectory so an answer can be explained by the path that produced it rather than only by its final citations.",
