      "The opening question is no longer which preference algorithm to use; it is whether a program can decide that the answer is right, because a unit test and a reward model are different objects with different infrastructure behind them and different ways of being gamed. Everything downstream follows from that answer - whether you operate a served reward-model fleet or a sandboxed grader pool, whether reward hacking looks like confident verbose prose or like a solution that special-cases the checker - and none of it is reproducible unless every rollout pins the policy, the tokenizer, the tool environment, and the grader version it was scored under.",
